${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229174 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f30ca427-3074-56c3-828a-1327d7b172b0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Effects of Vertical Shifting and Horizontal Reflection on Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e23fa1-6da3-572e-bd66-77a8a601e92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}_{5}-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417626} {:answer/id #uuid "653fa5e3-ca19-53d8-8efc-1572101bab0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}_{5}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417633} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee119c9-34d9-54bb-95d1-897eee545a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}_{5}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417640} {:answer/id #uuid "a55df8d9-38e8-5601-b8d7-deef23e1b3e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417646} {:answer/id #uuid "cd941ba1-1704-5611-ad32-048531d05e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}_{5}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55df8d9-38e8-5601-b8d7-deef23e1b3e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417646} :answer-field/id #uuid "740d0965-87e7-5216-9610-1dfafc6b0bb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dba35b3-124d-5cf7-913a-b8717960d9c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-340953" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th sine Fourier coefficient of $f(x)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that if $C$ is a constant, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(x)+C$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}+2C$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ${b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nIn our case, $C=5.$ Therefore, the $5$th sine coefficient of $f(x)+5$ is ${b}_{5}$ (remains unchanged)."}]}
